A = [
dict(n=26, slug="a2a", title="How Do AI Agents Talk to Each Other?", topic="A2A (agent-to-agent)",
buzz='"A2A." "Agent-to-agent protocol." As companies build many AI agents, they need a way for one agent to work with another, even from a different company.',
hw='Your travel agent needs a visa agent\'s help. First, they swap business cards: "I\'m the visa agent, I can check requirements and book appointments, here\'s how to reach me." Then they hand over the task and get updates until it\'s done.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: **A2A** is a shared way for AI agents to introduce themselves, hand over tasks and report back.',
step='Each agent publishes a "card" describing what it can do and how to contact it. Another agent reads the card, sends a task, and gets progress updates and results in an agreed format, even if the two were built by different teams on different tools.\n\nGoogle introduced the Agent2Agent protocol in 2025, and it\'s now developed as an open project. It complements MCP: MCP connects an agent to tools and data (Series 11); A2A connects agents to other agents.',
met='Early enterprise tools where, say, a hiring agent asks a separate background-check agent to do its part.',
name='**A2A**, the **Agent2Agent protocol**.',
words=[("Agent card","an agent's public description: what it does, how to reach it."),("Protocol","an agreed set of rules for talking."),("Task hand-off","one agent passing work to another.")],
notit='A2A doesn\'t make agents trustworthy. Each agent still needs permissions and checks (Series 15 covers multi-agent pitfalls; Series 16 what agents are allowed to do).',
take=["Ask whether your agents need to talk to other teams' agents at all.","Treat another agent's output like any outside input: check it.","Know the difference: MCP for tools, A2A for agents."],
close="Agents swapping business cards, then handing over work.",
q="Will your agents need to talk to someone else's agents?",
scene='Your travel agent needs a visa agent\'s help.\nFirst, they swap business cards.',
hook="A2A is how AI agents swap business cards and hand over tasks.",
extra="MCP connects agents to tools. A2A connects agents to agents."),

dict(n=27, slug="computer-use", title="How Does an AI Use a Computer Like You?", topic="Computer use",
buzz='"Computer use." "Browser agents." AIs that click, scroll and type on a screen. It sounds like a ghost moving your mouse.',
hw='Ask an AI: "Fill in this expense form." It takes a screenshot, finds the "Amount" box, clicks it, types ₹1,180, takes another screenshot to check, and moves to the next box.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: **look at the screen, decide, click or type, look again**, in a loop.',
step='The AI is multimodal (write-up 22), so it can read a screenshot. It decides the next action, such as click at this spot or type this text, and a program carries it out. Then a new screenshot shows what changed.\n\nIt\'s slower than using an API, but it works with any app that has a screen, including old systems with no API at all.',
met='Browser assistants that book or fill in things for you, and computer-use features in AI tools from several major providers.',
name='**Computer use**, also called **GUI agents** or **browser agents**.',
words=[("GUI","graphical user interface: the buttons and boxes on a screen."),("Screenshot loop","look, act, look again."),("Sandbox","a safe, separate computer for the agent to work in.")],
notit='It\'s not a person; it can misclick, misread or be fooled by text on a web page. Run it in a sandbox, with confirmations before anything irreversible (Series 16).',
take=["Use computer use where no API exists, not where one does.","Never give it your main computer and logged-in accounts unsupervised.","Require a human yes before payments or submissions."],
close="Look, click, look again.",
q="Would you let an AI click around your computer?",
scene='"Fill in this expense form."\nThe AI takes a screenshot, finds the box, clicks, types.',
hook="Look, decide, click, look again. That's computer use.",
extra="It works on any app with a screen, slowly."),

dict(n=28, slug="world-models", title="Can an AI Imagine What Happens Next?", topic="World models",
buzz='"World models." Some researchers call them the next big step after chatbots. What\'s a "world" to an AI?',
hw='Throw a ball in your head. You can picture where it lands before it does. You have a little simulator of the world inside you.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: a **world model** is an AI that predicts what happens next in a scene: how things move, fall and react.',
step='Language models predict the next word. World models predict the next moment: the next video frame, or how a scene changes if a robot pushes a box.\n\nThey learn from huge amounts of video and interaction. Uses include training robots and self-driving systems in imagined worlds before the real one, and generating interactive game-like scenes.',
met='AI-generated videos where objects move realistically (and sometimes don\'t). Research demos of playable worlds generated by AI.',
name='**World models**.',
words=[("Simulation","imitating how the world behaves."),("Embodied AI","AI that acts in the physical world, like robots."),("Physics","how objects move, fall and collide.")],
notit='World models don\'t truly understand physics yet. Watch AI videos closely: hands merge, objects vanish, glasses refill themselves.',
take=["Watch this space for robotics and self-driving news.","Look for physics mistakes in AI video before trusting it.","Separate demos from products; many are early research."],
close="Not the next word. The next moment.",
q="Have you spotted a physics mistake in an AI video?",
scene='Throw a ball in your head.\nYou already know where it lands.',
hook="A world model is an AI that imagines what happens next.",
extra="Not the next word: the next moment."),

dict(n=29, slug="benchmarks", title="What Does \"Scored 90% on a Benchmark\" Mean?", topic="Benchmarks",
buzz='"Scores 90% on MMLU." "Tops the leaderboard." Every launch has a chart of benchmark scores. What are they testing?',
hw='A school exam: the same questions for every student, marked the same way, so you can compare results.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: a **benchmark** is a fixed exam for AI models. MMLU, for example, is thousands of multiple-choice questions across 57 subjects, from law to maths.',
step='Different benchmarks test different skills: general knowledge, maths, coding, following instructions, or real tasks like fixing bugs. Leaderboards also use people voting for the better of two answers.\n\nThe catch: if exam questions leak into training data, scores go up without real skill. Popular benchmarks also stop separating top models once everyone scores near 90%, so new, harder ones keep appearing.',
met='Launch charts showing one model beating another by a few points. Leaderboards ranking chatbots.',
name='**Benchmarks** and **leaderboards**.',
words=[("Benchmark","a fixed test set used to compare models."),("Contamination","test questions leaking into training data."),("Saturation","when top models all score so high the test stops being useful.")],
notit='A benchmark score isn\'t a prediction of how a model will do on your work. Your own test set matters more (Series 5 covers evals).',
take=["Read benchmark charts as hints, not verdicts.","Check which skill the benchmark tests and whether it matches yours.","Build a small test set from your real tasks and use it to choose."],
close="A benchmark is a school exam for AI. Your work is a different exam.",
q="Do you trust launch-day benchmark charts?",
scene='"Scored 90% on MMLU."\n90% of what?',
hook="A benchmark is a school exam for AI. Your work is a different exam.",
extra="Use the scores as hints, then test on your own tasks."),

dict(n=30, slug="hallucination", title="Why Does an AI Sound Sure When It's Wrong?", topic="Hallucination",
buzz='"The AI hallucinated." It\'s the most famous AI problem. The word makes it sound like the AI is seeing things.',
hw='Ask an AI for a book by a real author on a niche topic. It might name a plausible-sounding title that doesn\'t exist, with confidence.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: remember write-up 3. An AI writes by guessing **likely** next words. A likely-sounding answer and a true answer usually match. When they don\'t, you get a **hallucination**.',
step='The AI has no built-in "I don\'t know" signal. If its training text rarely covered something, it still produces the most likely-sounding words, and fluent text sounds sure.\n\nGiving it the right source to read, asking it to cite, and allowing "I\'m not sure" all help. (How this is handled in real systems is covered in #JustAddRAG 5.)',
met='Made-up citations, invented quotes, wrong dates said confidently, and fake legal cases that have landed lawyers in trouble.',
name='**Hallucination**, sometimes called **confabulation**.',
words=[("Hallucination","fluent output that isn't true or isn't supported."),("Grounding","making answers rely on a given source."),("Citation","pointing to where a claim came from.")],
notit='It isn\'t lying (there\'s no intent) and it isn\'t a rare bug. It comes from how next-word guessing works, so it can be reduced, not fully removed.',
take=["Check names, numbers, quotes and citations before using them.","Give the AI the source document instead of asking from memory.","Make \"I'm not sure\" an acceptable answer in your prompts."],
close="Likely-sounding isn't the same as true.",
q="What's the most confident wrong answer an AI has given you?",
scene='Ask an AI for a book on a niche topic.\nIt names a real-sounding title. It doesn\'t exist.',
hook="An AI guesses likely words. Likely isn't always true. That's a hallucination.",
extra="Check names, numbers and quotes."),

dict(n=31, slug="watermarking", title="Can You Tell If AI Wrote This?", topic="AI watermarking",
buzz='"AI watermarking." "Content credentials." With AI images and text everywhere, people want to know what\'s real. Can you actually tell?',
hw='Banknotes have a watermark: hold one up to the light and a hidden mark appears. Invisible normally, checkable when needed.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: an **AI watermark** is a hidden signal placed in AI-made images, audio or text that a checking tool can detect.',
step='For images and audio, the signal is hidden in tiny changes people can\'t see or hear. For text, the AI slightly prefers certain word choices in a pattern a detector can spot. Separately, "content credentials" attach a signed label saying how a file was made, like a receipt.\n\nGoogle\'s SynthID and the C2PA content credentials standard are two well-known examples.',
met='"AI-generated" labels on social media posts. Photo apps showing an info tag about how an image was made or edited.',
name='**AI watermarking** and **content credentials** (provenance).',
words=[("Watermark","a hidden, detectable signal in content."),("Provenance","the record of where content came from."),("Detector","a tool that checks for the signal.")],
notit='Watermarks aren\'t proof. Edits, cropping, re-typing or translating can weaken them, and "AI detectors" that guess from writing style are unreliable. No mark doesn\'t mean human-made.',
take=["Treat \"AI-generated\" labels as helpful, not final.","Don't judge people's work with AI-writing detectors alone.","Add content credentials to your company's official images where you can."],
close="A hidden mark, checkable with the right light.",
q="Should all AI content be labelled?",
scene='Hold a banknote up to the light.\nA hidden mark appears.',
hook="AI watermarks work the same way: invisible, until you check.",
extra="Helpful, but not proof."),

dict(n=32, slug="vibe-coding", title="Can You Build an App by Describing It?", topic="Vibe coding",
buzz='"Vibe coding." People build apps by chatting with an AI, without reading the code. Developers argue about it. Leaders wonder if it changes hiring.',
hw='Say to an AI tool: "Make me a page where my family can vote on dinner, with three options and a big result." A minute later, it works. You never looked at the code.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: **describe what you want, test the result, ask for changes**, and let the AI handle the code.',
step='AI coding tools write, run and fix code from plain-language requests. You steer by trying the app and saying "make the button bigger" or "it crashes when I click twice".\n\nThe term was coined by AI researcher Andrej Karpathy in early 2025. It\'s great for prototypes, personal tools and learning. For real products, someone still has to understand the code: security, data, costs and fixes don\'t vibe themselves.',
met='Non-programmers building small apps with AI app builders, and developers using AI agents to write most of a feature.',
name='**Vibe coding**.',
words=[("Prompt","the plain-language request you give the AI."),("Prototype","a quick working version to try an idea."),("Technical debt","shortcuts in code that cost you later.")],
notit='Vibe coding isn\'t "no engineering needed". The easy 80% gets faster; the last 20% (security, scale, maintenance) still needs people who read the code.',
take=["Use it to prototype ideas in hours, not weeks.","Have someone review anything that touches real users, money or data.","Keep the prototype and the production version as separate decisions."],
close="Describe it, try it, fix it. Then have someone read the code.",
q="Have you built something without writing code?",
scene='"Make me a page where my family can vote on dinner."\nA minute later, it works.',
hook="Describe it, test it, ask for changes. That's vibe coding.",
extra="Great for prototypes. Real products still need someone who reads the code."),
]
